⏺ This is a good design problem. Let me reason through the mechanics first, then
   offer concrete directions.
     
  What actually happens today:

  For the two_b7 spread-Z, both H stubs live on the same layer (M4) and their
  routing-direction spans are adjacent, not overlapping ([-50,75] and [75,200]).
   So NUTS's first-fit places them both at y=420 (interval bottom), the trunk's
  effective length collapses to zero, and the result is already optimal. The
  problem you're describing — paying for a full [420,580] trunk in congestion
  accounting even when the stubs can meet — is real but the placement itself is
  already good here.

  The interesting general case is when the stubs have partially overlapping
  intervals and first-fit doesn't naturally bring them together:

  stub A interval: [100, 300]   first-fit → 100
  stub B interval: [200, 400]   first-fit → 200          
  trunk effective length: |100 - 200| = 100
  optimal: both at 200 → trunk = 0

  Three ideas, roughly ranked by tractability:

  ---
  1. Post-NUTS trunk-squeeze pass (local search)
  
  After the per-layer first-fit placement, run a second pass over connected
  stub–trunk–stub triples. For each triple:
  - Compute the midpoint of the two stubs' current track positions.
  - Try sliding each stub toward that midpoint inside its interval, checking
  only the segments whose spans it actually overlaps on that layer. 
  - Accept any move that reduces trunk effective length without creating a new
  conflict.
  - Iterate to convergence (usually 1–2 rounds).
  
  This fits cleanly into the existing architecture — it's a post-processing step
   inside NUTSEngine::run(), and per-layer independence is preserved. The
  triples can be identified from the topology's seg_busterms map (the trunk
  segment already knows which block faces it connects to) or from ConnTopology.
  The main bookkeeping addition: the trunk TrackSegment needs to record which
  two stubs determine its effective span, so the output can report accurate wire
   length.

  ---
  2. Preferred-position hint on stubs
  
  Add an optional preferred_pos field to TrackSegment. In first-fit, try
  preferred_pos as a candidate before interval_lo. For spread-Z stubs, the
  topology generator would set preferred_pos = (interval_lo + interval_hi) / 2 —
   both stubs would converge naturally to the center in first-fit without
  touching the trunk logic at all.
                                                         
  Cheaper to implement, but less general: it only works when you can precompute
  the ideal position at topology-generation time, and it doesn't adapt to other
  segments on the same layer that might already occupy the center.
  
  ---                                                    
  3. Telescope the trunk span in the topology itself
  
  Instead of representing the trunk as a fixed-span segment [y1, y2], represent
  it as a zero-span "stub connector" at the nominal position. Generate the
  spread-Z with the trunk initialised to a degenerate span [center, center],
  letting the stubs' actual Hanan-interval constraints expand it only if forced
  to. This changes the wirelength estimate immediately (the planner would
  correctly rank it cheaper) and removes the congestion inflation on the V
  layer.

  The trade-off: it requires a new segment kind or a flag in TrackSegment, and
  NUTS needs to handle the "telescoping" span during sweep. More architectural
  change than ideas 1 or 2.
  
  ---                                                    
  Which to pursue:
  
  Idea 1 is the most general and most architecturally honest — trunk length
  really is a cross-layer derived quantity, and a post-pass is the right place
  to handle it. Ideas 2 and 3 bake in assumptions at topology-generation time
  and will miss cases the topology generator can't anticipate.
  
  The one missing piece for idea 1: a way for the NUTS engine to know which two
  stubs determine a trunk's span. The cleanest carrier for that is the
  TrackSegment — add a coupled_seg_indices: pair<int,int> field that the CLI
  populates from ConnTopology when building the NUTS input. The post-pass then
  has everything it needs without any geometric re-analysis.

